<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imperial-Royal Landwehr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as k.k. Landwehr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Imperial-Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landwehr">Landwehr</a> was the territorial army of the Austrian half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, created in 1869 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a> alongside the joint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-and-royal-army">Imperial and Royal Army</a> and the Hungarian Honvéd. At first a reserve force, it became a full front-line army before 1914 and fought throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imperial-royal-landwehr.jpg" alt="Imperial-Royal Landwehr" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felipe Fidelis Tobias · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Regimental_Flag_of_the_Imperial-Royal_Landwehr,_Cerimonial_Flag_of_the_Gardebataillon_and_Standard_of_the_Common_Army_(Obverse).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Imperial-Royal might seem to join the two halves of the monarchy, but here Royal refers to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, a crownland of the Austrian half to whose kingship the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> remained formally entitled. Calls on all able-bodied men to defend the country go back to the 16th century, and an imperial decree of 9 June 1808 created a standing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landwehr">Landwehr</a> to complement the regular army; it was used in 1809 and in 1813/14 and abolished in 1859. Beethoven's March No. 1 of 1808, written for the Bohemian Landwehr, is now known as the Yorck March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the Compromise gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> the right to its own Royal Hungarian Landwehr, the Honvéd, and the Austrian Landwehr was set up as its counterpart in the kingdoms and lands represented in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsrat">Reichsrat</a>. The Defence Act of 1889 tasked it in war with supporting the army and defending the homeland and, by exception in peacetime, with keeping order, and set its yearly intake at 10,000 men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">Conscripts</a> served from 21 to 32, two or three years on active duty, and then passed to the Landsturm militia.</p><p>In 1914 it had 37 infantry regiments, 6 regiments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">lancers</a>, 8 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">field artillery</a> and 8 field howitzer battalions, and mountain troops that included four Tyrolean rifle regiments, renamed Kaiserschützen in January 1917. On 11 April 1917 its infantry units were renamed Schützen, rifles. In September 1915 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph I</a> granted it colours for its achievements in the war, to be presented after the war; they were never issued under the monarchy, and only later passed to traditional units, as those of the 2nd Linz regiment did on 8 June 1924.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landwehr">Landwehr</a> reported to the Imperial-Royal Ministry of Defence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, independent of the joint Ministry of War, and had its own barracks, depots and officer training; its officers trained at the Franz Joseph Military Academy in Vienna. Its regiments had three battalions instead of the four of the Common Army but were often better equipped, since the parliaments were readier to fund their own troops. Each bore the name of its staff's garrison town, as in the 6th Eger Landwehr Infantry Regiment, and its uniforms are shown in the Military History Museum in Vienna.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Imperial-Royal Landwehr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The common k.u.k. army (kaiserlich und königlich, Imperial and Royal), alongside the separate <b>Austrian Landwehr</b> and Hungarian Honvéd, drew conscripts from all the empire's peoples, and its regiments were so linguistically mixed that officers were expected to master the languages of their men.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
